[Music]  Senator Michael Bennet of Colorado has  served in the US Senate since 2009 he  has also been one of the Democrats  considering a run for president in 2020  senator Bennet joins us now only on CBS  this morning to announce his plans for  the future good morning senator morning  so what are your plans you know my plan  is to run for president I appreciate  your letting me come here to announce  that I think this country faces two  enormous challenges among others one is  a lack of economic mobility and  opportunity for most Americans and the  other is the need to restore integrity  to our government I need I think we need  to do both of those things and I've  spent the last couple of years writing a  book about our politics over the last 10  years or so and I became convinced in  that process that if we continue to go  down the path we're going and this isn't  just about President Trump it's about  the politics that existed before he got  there he certainly has made matters  worse but if we keep going down this  road we're gonna be the first generation  of Americans to leave less opportunity  not more to the next generation and I I  just need to do everything I can do to  make sure that we don't do that you  recently got a prostate cancer diagnosis  and you said that made you want to do  bigger things is this decision a part of  that it was it was very clarifying you  know I I feel incredibly lucky because I  got a diagnosis in five years later  after an operation I was cleared with a  clean bill of health  that gave me a chance to think about  whether I really wanted to run or not  and in the in the context of getting the  diagnosis  my reaction was which disappointment at  the idea I couldn't run it would have  been a great excuse to say this is just  some adolescent fantasy and I shouldn't  run that's not how I reacted and frankly  the other reaction I had was to think  about what it would feel like to get a  diagnosis like that and to not have  insurance and to know that this  president has worked so hard since he's  been president to take insurance away  from people in America let me quick  political question before we go back to  Paula - but before we go back to policy  there are 20 some-odd candidates that we  don't can't even fix on a proper number  you're not  you're not the only person from Colorado  who's running right so John Hickenlooper  running as well for whom you used to  work so in the in the business of  politics how do you distinguish yourself  from all the other well you know what I  called my mom and I said to her somebody  has to be 22 and that's why I'm running  and it turns out I'm not I'm 21 so I've  already made a little bit of progress I  think I distinguish myself in a number  of ways one is I have a tendency to tell  the truth the people I represent in  Colorado and want the chance to do that  with the American people  second I've won very tough races in a  purple state winning red counties in  that state and third I've got a track  record of bipartisan work that's been  harder to do during the Trump  administration but still over the 10  years that I've been in the Senate and  then before that a track record in  business turning around failing  businesses and working for five years  for the children in the Denver Public  Schools as their superintendent I don't  think anybody has his broadest set of  experiences in the field and I think  that will distinguish me let me ask you  you want to focus on health care but you  talk about bipartisan working in washing  there are a lot of Democrats who think  that bipartisanship doesn't exist in  Washington anymore yeah that the  candidate who's the nominee of the party  has to participate in politics as they  are not as they should be in some dream  about bipartisanship that doesn't exist  yeah I think that's even more naive than  then believing in some dream that  doesn't exist in other words it's naive  when you're at when you're when you're  considering whether you're gonna deliver  for the next generation of Americans  there is no bipartisanship in Washington  today to speak up that is true and we  have been terrorized for the last ten  years by the freedom caucus these people  won't cooperate they won't compromise  they believe they have a monopoly on  wisdom and they've got a cartoon version  of what the Founding Fathers were trying  to do they need to be beaten or they  need to be closed over but I come from  and they and I come from a state that's  1/3 Democratic 1/3 independent and a  third Republican the people I work with  are not like the freedom caucus they are  Americans who are trying to drive  opportunity for their families and for  the next generation and they need a  politics that recognizes that they have  disagreements among themselves but they  have a shared there's  desire to move the country forward we  have drained our politics of that in  Washington our politics in Washington  lack all imagination and I think going  down the rat hole with the people that  are dragging us down the rat hole just  as a self-fulfilling prophecy of failing  as a republic that's what I really  believe that's what I think is at stake  given the number of Democrats that are  running for president is that a sign of  a healthy party or a party who doesn't  know what it what it really well I think  it's both I think I think the Democratic  right now the Democratic Party doesn't  stand for very much at the national  level with respect to what the American  people think but this is the opportunity  for us to show what we stand for for us  to have a competition of ideas and I  think it's phenomenal that we've got as  diverse array of candidates as we have  in all respects and that we've got the  number that we have you know we it's a  process like this is long overdue in the  Democratic Party what's the other big  policy idea there should be a  competition about well I think another  welcome in health care well to me it's  economic mobility how do we restore an  economy where when but the president  would say the economy is doing great  yeah he would say that let me say a  couple things about that first of all if  you look at a chart of the jobs created  since onine which is that the depths of  the recession when Barack Obama took  over until today the chart goes like  this every single every single year it's  gone like this president Trump's been  present since here so I will give him  credit for not screwing up what was  already happening before he got there  but from the vantage point of people in  our country who can't afford housing  can't afford health care can't afford  early childhood education can't afford  higher education can't afford a  middle-class life they need us to  restore economic mobility in this  country and that is what we should be  focused on in this campaign all right  we're gonna have to leave it there  thanks for being with us he's running

